From a8210156fb2dfda00d4ec449fe29ccfb1d9ee6ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 21:55:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] USB audio: the song's name and the firmware for a recorder on
 the computer (SysEx 0x12 on port 3)

While the computer has the USB audio stream open, the Deluge tells its song's
name and its firmware on USB MIDI port 3, the port for SysEx that DAWs leave
alone: F0 00 21 7B 01 12 <{"song":"...","fw":"..."}: JSON, UTF-8, packed 7
bytes into 8 as in util/pack.c> F7. At once when the stream starts and when
the song's name changes, else every 2 s, so a recorder that starts listening
later learns it too. Sent only, never answered; only with room in the USB MIDI
send buffer (as the CPU monitor's SysEx).

DelugeRec (tools/deluge_rec.py, v5 and later) listens there and names its
files "<song> <date> <time> <firmware>.WAV", with the full firmware in the
file's RIFF INFO. The song is currentSong->name ("" for a new song not saved
yet), converted from CP437 (FatFs's code page) to UTF-8, at most 120
characters and 240 bytes, cut at a whole character; the firmware is
kFirmwareVersionString.

The message is built in usb_song_info_message.cpp without any hardware, so it
can be built and tested on a computer against the recorder's parser.
